  "title": "Commands as an object map",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "md": "## A table of functions\n\n`const cmds = { go(dir) { Player.move(dir) }, turn() { Player.rotate(90) } }` stores functions under names. You look up a name and call it. That is how a small language of stage commands stays data.\n\nThe name without `()` is the function value. You still have to call it.\n\nThe desk files `go` and `turn` on a clipboard. The fox only moves when you say `cmds.go(\"east\")`, not when you glance at `cmds.go`."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "id": "lookup-call",
      "promptMd": "`cmds.go` without `()` is…",
      "kind": "mcq",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "move",
          "md": "A move that already happened"
        },
        {
          "id": "fn",
          "md": "The function value — you still have to call it"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "fn"
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "id": "lookup-kind",
      "kind": "cloze",
      "promptMd": "`cmds.go` without `()` is {{a}}.",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "the function value",
              "md": "the function value"
            },
            {
              "id": "a finished move",
              "md": "a finished move"
            },
            {
              "id": "undefined",
              "md": "undefined"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answer": {
        "a": "the function value"
      },
      "explainMd": "Looking up the name gives you the function. Parentheses are what run it."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "id": "cmd-table",
      "engine": "javascript",
      "entry": "main.js",
      "promptMd": "> Build `cmds.go` / `cmds.turn`. go east three times, turn, stand on (3,0) facing 90°.",
